    id: 'kyodai-on-shuffling-cards',
    title: 'On Shuffling Cards',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "In the house where I grew up, card games were played on winter evenings at the kitchen table, and the person who shuffled was always my uncle. He had a way of splitting the deck into two halves, bending them slightly and letting the cards fall together in a soft, rapid patter. Then he pushed the two halves into one, tapped the edges straight and set the deck down in the middle of the table. As a child I thought this was the most impressive thing an adult could do. Only much later did I begin to wonder why it mattered so much to all of us.",
      "A shuffle, after all, produces nothing that anyone can see, since a deck that has been shuffled looks exactly like a deck that has not. Its value lies entirely in what has been destroyed, namely the order left behind by the previous game. Cards that were gathered up in sets and runs must be scattered again, so that no player can remember where anything is. Without this step, a skilled player could follow the cards from one game to the next and know what the others were holding. Shuffling is therefore an act of deliberate forgetting, performed so that the next game can start without a memory of the last.",
      "It is also, I think, a promise of fairness. Before the cards are dealt, everyone at the table agrees to accept whatever arrives. The player who receives a poor hand has no grounds for complaint, because nobody chose it for them. This is why a careless shuffle irritates people far more than a losing game does. A loss under fair conditions is simply bad luck, but a game in which the cards were not properly mixed feels slightly ( A ), even if nobody intended any harm.",
      "Mathematicians have taken this question seriously. They have shown that a few casual shuffles leave a surprising amount of the old order in place. Dave Bayer and Persi Diaconis famously found that, by one common measure, about seven riffle shuffles of the kind my uncle performed are needed to mix a standard deck well. I find this oddly comforting, because it suggests that forgetting is real work, and that the past does not disappear simply because we have decided to move on. A few quick movements may leave the old pattern almost intact beneath a fresh appearance.",
      "The same is true, I suspect, outside card games. When a team is reorganized or a family moves to a new town, people often expect a fresh start to arrive at once. Yet old habits and old alliances tend to survive the first rearrangement, just as pairs of cards survive a single shuffle. It takes repeated mixing, and some patience, before a group can behave as though the previous game has truly ended. Newcomers often notice this sooner than anyone else, because they can still see the old groups that the others have stopped noticing. Pretending that one shuffle is enough usually leads to disappointment.",
      "There is, however, another side to the matter, since not everything in life should be shuffled away. Some orders are worth keeping, such as the sequence of letters in a word or the pages of a book, and we take great care to protect them. Card players themselves sort their hands as soon as the cards are dealt, putting suits together and ranking them from high to low. The shuffle and the sorting belong together, since we break up an order only so that a new one can be built.",
      "I still own an old deck of cards that belonged to my uncle, its corners soft and its pictures faded. When I shuffle it, I cannot manage the soft patter he produced, and the cards tend to stick together in awkward clumps. Sometimes half the deck sprays across the floor, and whoever is playing with me laughs as I crawl around collecting it. But I keep trying, because the action carries a meaning that seems larger than any single game. Each shuffle is a small agreement that the past will not decide what happens next. It reminds me that luck, at least at the kitchen table, is something people can choose to share fairly.",
    ],
