      "In seventeenth-century London, one of the most anxiously read documents was a single printed sheet that appeared every week. It listed the number of people who had been buried in each parish of the city during the previous seven days, together with the number of children baptized. It also gave the causes of the deaths, from fevers and old age to accidents and plague. These sheets were known as the bills of mortality, and for many Londoners they were the best available guide to whether the city was becoming dangerous.",
      "The bills grew out of fears of plague. Lists of deaths were produced occasionally in the sixteenth century, and from the early seventeenth century they were issued every week as a regular series. The work was organized by the Company of Parish Clerks, whose members collected the figures from their own parishes. Each parish clerk reported the burials and baptisms recorded in the parish, and the totals were combined and printed. Readers could buy the bills or receive them by subscription, and many studied them closely, comparing one week with the next.",
      "The information about causes of death came from people known as searchers. They were usually elderly women appointed by the parish, and their task was to visit the body of anyone who had died and report what had caused the death. They had no medical training, and their judgments often rested on what relatives told them or on what they could see. Searchers could also be persuaded to record a less frightening cause, because a household where plague had been reported might be shut up. As a result, the figures for particular diseases were never entirely trustworthy.",
      "Even so, the bills had great practical importance. When the number of plague deaths began to rise, wealthy families who could afford to leave the city often did so. Those who had fled then watched the weekly totals for signs that it was safe to return. During the great outbreak of 1665, nearly seventy thousand deaths from plague were recorded in the bills. Historians generally believe that the true number was considerably higher, since many deaths were never reported or were assigned to other causes.",
      "The bills became the basis of a new kind of knowledge. In 1662, a London merchant named John Graunt published a short book in which he examined decades of figures from the bills. Instead of treating each death as a separate event, he looked for regular patterns in large numbers. He noticed, for example, that slightly more boys than girls were baptized each year, and that the proportion of deaths from some causes was remarkably ( A ) from year to year. He also attempted to estimate the population of London and the share of children who survived to adulthood.",
      "Graunt's methods were simple, and many of his estimates were rough, but his approach proved enormously influential. He showed that events which seem entirely unpredictable for any single person can follow stable patterns across a whole population. For this reason, he is often regarded as one of the founders of demography and of modern statistics. His work also encouraged others to use similar figures to calculate the prices of annuities and other financial products that depended on how long people lived.",
      "The bills themselves had serious limitations. They counted burials in the churchyards of the established church, so people buried elsewhere, including members of some other religious groups, were often missing. The area they covered was also fixed, while London grew far beyond it. In the nineteenth century they were gradually replaced by a national system of civil registration, introduced in England and Wales in 1837, which recorded births, marriages and deaths directly. Yet the idea behind the bills, that counting deaths carefully can reveal how a society lives, remains at the heart of public health today.",
