    id: 'adv-venetian-arsenal-shipyard',
    title: 'A Shipyard Run by a State: The Arsenal of Venice',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "For centuries, the Republic of Venice was one of the richest powers in Europe, although it controlled little land. Its wealth came from the sea, from trade with the eastern Mediterranean and from the ships that protected that trade. At the heart of this maritime power stood the Arsenal, a vast state-owned complex where warships and merchant galleys were built, repaired and stored. Surrounded by high walls, it is traditionally said to have been founded in the early twelfth century. Over the following centuries, it grew into what is often described as one of the largest industrial sites in Europe before the age of factories.",
      "Unlike most shipbuilding of the period, the work at the Arsenal was organized by the government rather than by private owners. The state paid the workers, bought timber, hemp and iron, and decided how many ships were needed. Its workers, known as arsenalotti, formed a large and proud community, and some families worked there for generations. Historians differ on their exact number, but at its height the Arsenal is often said to have employed several thousand people.",
      "What made the Arsenal famous was the speed with which it could produce ships. Instead of building each vessel from start to finish in one place, workers specialized in particular tasks, and parts such as frames, oars and fittings were made to standard patterns. Hulls could be kept in a partly finished state and completed quickly when war threatened. Pero Tafur, a Spanish traveler who visited in 1436, described galleys being towed along a canal past a row of windows, from which workers handed out ropes, weapons and supplies. By the time a galley reached the end, it was ready to ( A ).",
      "For this reason, some historians describe the Arsenal as an early example of the methods later used in modern factories. The comparison should not be pushed too far, since most of the work was still done by hand and the pace depended on skilled workers rather than machines. A famous story claims that in 1574, while the visiting King Henry III of France was at a banquet, the workers assembled an entire galley. The story may well be exaggerated, but it shows the reputation that the Arsenal enjoyed.",
      "The Arsenal also left a mark on literature. In the early fourteenth century, the poet Dante compared a lake of boiling pitch in the twenty-first canto of his Inferno to the pitch boiled in the Arsenal in winter. Workers used it to seal the seams of damaged ships. The comparison suggests how familiar the noise, smoke and smell of the shipyard had become. Supplying such work required enormous quantities of materials. Venice protected forests on the mainland to secure timber for its ships, and ropes were made in a very long building within the walls.",
      "From the sixteenth century onward, the Arsenal's importance slowly declined. The main routes of world trade shifted toward the Atlantic, and the oared galley, which the Arsenal had been designed to produce, gave way to large sailing ships armed with many guns. Venice adapted, but it could no longer compete with the growing naval powers of western Europe. When the Republic fell to Napoleon's forces in 1797, the Arsenal was partly destroyed. Later governments used it as a naval base, and parts of it are still used by the Italian navy today.",
      "In recent decades, some of the old buildings have found a new purpose as spaces for international exhibitions of art and architecture. Visitors who walk through them can still see the long halls where galleys were once built side by side. The Arsenal reminds us that the organization of work can be as important as any invention. Venice did not possess a secret technology, but it learned to combine planning, standard parts and skilled labor on a scale that few rivals could match.",
    ],
