    id: 'todai-official-lunar-time',
    title: 'Should the Moon Have Its Own Official Time?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "For more than half a century, astronauts and spacecraft near the Moon have relied on time kept on Earth. Missions were short, and controllers on the ground could correct small timing errors from home. That situation is now changing, as space agencies and private companies plan landers, satellites and eventually long-term bases on and around the Moon. In April 2024, the White House Office of Science and Technology Policy directed NASA to develop what it called Coordinated Lunar Time. The agency was reportedly asked to produce a plan by the end of 2026, working with other parts of the government and with international partners.",
      "The need for such a standard comes partly from physics. According to Einstein's theory of general relativity, clocks run slightly faster where gravity is weaker. The Moon's gravity is much weaker than Earth's, so a clock on its surface gains on an identical clock on Earth. Once other effects are included, such as the slight slowing caused by the Moon's motion, the net gain is about fifty-six microseconds a day. That sounds trivial, but radio signals used for navigation travel about three hundred meters in a single microsecond. Some estimates suggest that, if the drift were simply ignored, positions calculated from such signals could be off by more than fifteen kilometers within a day.",
      "Supporters of an official lunar time argue that it is essential for safety. In coming decades, spacecraft from several countries may be landing in the same regions of the Moon, especially around its south pole. If each mission kept time in its own way, their clocks would soon become ( A ) with one another, and a small mismatch might send a rover or lander to the wrong place. A common standard would allow navigation satellites around the Moon to work much as similar systems around the Earth do today. Supporters compare it to the adoption of standard time zones on Earth, which helped make railroad timetables reliable.",
      "Supporters also argue that the timing of the decision matters. Rules are far easier to agree on before many systems are built than after each country has invested in its own. In 2024, the International Astronomical Union passed a resolution recommending a standard system of reference for the Moon, including a lunar time scale. Space agencies in Europe and elsewhere have been discussing similar ideas, and supporters hope that these efforts can be combined into one shared standard. Without such cooperation, they warn, the Moon could end up with several incompatible clocks, each favored by a different group of nations.",
      "Skeptics agree that clocks on the Moon behave differently, but they question whether a new official time is the best answer. The difference of fifty-six microseconds a day is well understood and can be calculated precisely. Computers aboard spacecraft could simply keep Earth's time and apply the correction automatically, as satellite navigation systems already do for their own relativistic effects. Creating a separate time scale, skeptics say, adds a layer of complexity that every engineer will have to manage. Each conversion between two systems is also one more chance for an error to creep in.",
      "Other skeptics worry less about the physics than about politics. The directive asked one national agency to develop the standard, and some observers fear that a single country could shape rules that others are then expected to follow. Skeptics also note that a lunar day, from one sunrise to the next, lasts about twenty-nine and a half Earth days, so lunar time would mean little to the people living there. Astronauts would still sleep and work according to the hours of a day on Earth. In practice, official lunar time would matter mainly to their machines.",
      "I believe that the Moon does need its own time scale, but only if it is created through genuine international agreement. The skeptics are right that the physics is simple and that conversions add risk. Yet a correction that every mission applies in its own way is exactly the kind of small inconsistency that grows dangerous as traffic increases. A single standard, closely linked to the time kept on Earth and managed by a body in which many nations take part, seems the safer path. The worst outcome would not be a new clock, but several rival clocks competing for the same sky.",
    ],
